<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Can I Learn From Other Cultures? – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate informational content from various sources. Reading: Summarize discussions from academic texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate information from travel articles and summarize cultural learning experiences related to other countries (e.g., "I read an article comparing the preservation efforts at the National Museum of Archaeology and Ethnology in Guatemala City and the Ixchel Museum of Indigenous Textiles and Clothing. Both emphasize the importance of preserving Mayan history and cultural identity.")<br>Can summarize discussions on intercultural exchange in academic texts (e.g., "The text explained that travel promotes global understanding by exposing people to different cultures, allowing them to experience new perspectives and challenge their own assumptions.")<br>Can discuss insights gained from informational texts on global travel (e.g., "The article suggests that learning a second language while traveling opens up new opportunities for communication, helps travelers understand local customs, and enhances their overall cultural experience.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting diverse cultural scenes (e.g., traditional festivals, famous landmarks like the Great Pyramids or a Japanese temple, diverse culinary dishes, people wearing traditional clothing). Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what they see and what comes to mind when they think about "global community" and "other cultures." The teacher then prompts a whole-class discussion with questions like: "What do these pictures make you curious about?" or "If you could visit any of these places, which one would you choose and why?" This activates prior knowledge and introduces the theme and scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target vocabulary and grammar: "If I could travel anywhere, I would visit Egypt to see the ancient pyramids. It would be an unforgettable experience to explore such a unique culture." The teacher then highlights the pronunciation of "could" and "would" with vowel reduction (/kəd/, /wəd/) and emphasizes the stress on multisyllabic words like "adVENture" and "des-ti-NA-tion" when pointing to specific images (e.g., a challenging hike: "This looks like an amazing adVENture!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'unforgettable experience', does it mean it's easy to forget or impossible to forget?" (Impossible to forget!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'diverse cultures' mean? Are all the cultures exactly the same?" (No, they are different, varied!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive an informational travel article titled "Global Journeys: Learning from Diverse Traditions" which describes two contrasting travel experiences focused on cultural learning. The article includes descriptions of various destinations, mentions an Exchange Program, and incorporates the target grammar (second conditional, superlatives, past continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., *breathtaking, global, unique, explore, understand, traditions, diversity, benefits, perspectives*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student evaluates informational content from travel articles to understand different cultural learning experiences and perspectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly and answer: "What is the main idea the author wants to convey about cultural travel?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements and determine if they are true or false based on the text. For example: "The article suggests that learning a new language has no benefits for travelers." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match a cultural learning experience (e.g., "experiencing local festivals") with the specific destination or type of travel (e.g., "a year-long Exchange Program in Asia") described in the article.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice evaluating and summarizing information from the article, focusing on the correct use and identification of the second conditional for hypothetical travel, superlatives for comparing places, and past continuous to narrate past experiences, along with the target vocabulary related to travel and culture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Grammar Hunt:</b> Students re-read the article individually and identify specific grammatical structures. They underline all sentences that use the second conditional (e.g., "If I could go anywhere..."), circle all superlatives (e.g., "the most beautiful city"), and highlight verbs in the past continuous (e.g., "While traveling, I was learning new cultures."). The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Vocabulary & Contextual Inference:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences extracted from the article, where target vocabulary words are replaced with blanks. Students fill in the blanks using a word bank (e.g., *diversity, breathtaking, understand, enhance, perspectives, traditions*). For example: "The article highlighted the cultural ______ found in Southeast Asia." Students also practice reading aloud selected sentences containing modal verbs "could/would" and multisyllabic words like "adVENture," emphasizing correct stress patterns as modeled by the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Summarizing Details:</b> In pairs, students discuss specific sections of the article, summarizing key information and cultural learning points. They use prompts like: "According to the article, what was the most enriching benefit of the Exchange Program?" (uses superlative) or "If you were to encounter a tradition mentioned in the text, how would you react?" (uses second conditional). Students are encouraged to use phrases like: "What is one tradition you found interesting while reading?" to respectfully discuss cultural differences and travel experiences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion & Collaborative Summary Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group's task is to discuss and evaluate the informational content from the travel article and then prepare a short, collaborative oral summary (3-4 minutes) to present to the class. The summary should focus on the insights gained about cultural learning and global understanding, addressing the question: "What can we learn from other cultures, as presented in the article?" Groups must use at least one example of the second conditional, superlatives, and past continuous in their summary, along with a range of target vocabulary (e.g., *diversity, experience, understanding, perspectives, benefits, enhance*). They are encouraged to respectfully discuss cultural differences and show interest in each other's contributions (e.g., "How did the text change your perspective on travel?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Sociolinguistic Focus:</b> Students lead group discussions to explore cultural diversity and the personal benefits of travel from the text, ensuring everyone has input and contributes to the final summary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist (focused on group presentation and discussion participation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their collaborative summary of the travel article, highlighting key cultural learning experiences and insights.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to evaluate informational content from the travel article and summarize it clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Coherent and logical presentation of cultural learning experiences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of second conditional, superlatives, and past continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., *diversity, understanding, perspectives, enhance, benefits, traditions*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation and respectful discussion within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, including correct stress on multisyllabic words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new cultural insight I gained from reading today's article is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it (easy/challenging) to identify and use the second conditional when discussing hypothetical travel. Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific information from the article surprised you or made you think differently about global travel?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has today's reading activity helped you to better understand the importance of cultural diversity?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading comprehension activities worked well, particularly the grammar hunt and vocabulary fill-in-the-blanks, which helped reinforce language accuracy. The group discussions for the summary were generally productive, though some groups needed more scaffolding to ensure equal participation. Students demonstrated good understanding of the main ideas and were able to apply the target grammar in their summaries. For the next lesson, I will ensure more explicit modeling of interactive phrases to encourage deeper peer discussion and prepare them for actively speaking and interacting using the target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on evaluating and summarizing informational content. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact in pairs, sharing their personal travel dreams and hypothetical cultural experiences based on the knowledge gained from the reading, using the second conditional, superlatives, and past continuous in communicative tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>